WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:02.330
- [Woman] This Great Big
Story was made possible

00:00:02.330 --> 00:00:05.263
by P&amp;G, Love Over Bias.

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:09.420
- [Man] Bayard Rustin was
one of the most important

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:11.530
figures in the African American struggle

00:00:11.530 --> 00:00:13.120
for civil rights and freedom.

00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:16.210
He organized the 1963 March on Washington.

00:00:16.210 --> 00:00:19.357
He helped Dr. King
translate the philosophy

00:00:19.357 --> 00:00:21.630
of nonviolence into direct action.

00:00:21.630 --> 00:00:23.410
But a lot of people don't know his name,

00:00:23.410 --> 00:00:25.060
largely because he was a gay man.

00:00:29.940 --> 00:00:31.160
- My name is Walter Naegle,

00:00:31.160 --> 00:00:34.023
and Bayard Rustin was the love of my life.

00:00:34.890 --> 00:00:38.670
I met Bayard on a corner at
42nd Street and 7th Avenue,

00:00:38.670 --> 00:00:41.170
Times Square in 1977.

00:00:41.170 --> 00:00:44.304
I was 27 years old, and he was 65.

00:00:44.304 --> 00:00:48.710
There was this tall,
handsome, very attractive man

00:00:48.710 --> 00:00:51.190
standing next to me and
we looked at each other,

00:00:51.190 --> 00:00:53.243
and I was pretty much toast.

00:00:54.820 --> 00:00:57.540
Bayard was open about his being gay,

00:00:57.540 --> 00:00:59.710
really, from his teenage years.

00:00:59.710 --> 00:01:02.430
He just believed in
being his authentic self

00:01:02.430 --> 00:01:03.933
and speaking his own truth.

00:01:06.799 --> 00:01:09.560
Bayard always said that
Dr. King didn't really

00:01:09.560 --> 00:01:12.280
have a personal problem
with him being gay.

00:01:12.280 --> 00:01:14.950
Dr. King really needed
Bayard at different times,

00:01:14.950 --> 00:01:17.520
because Bayard was a
person of great creativity,

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:20.090
great intellect, and he was doing things

00:01:20.090 --> 00:01:23.280
for the movement that
really nobody else could do.

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:24.940
After the March on Washington,

00:01:24.940 --> 00:01:27.250
he became a much more visible figure.

00:01:27.250 --> 00:01:30.480
He was really out there in
the thick of the movement.

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:32.600
- We're interested in making it possible

00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:35.063
for people to live like human beings.

00:01:35.063 --> 00:01:38.637
About the so-called destructive child.

00:01:38.637 --> 00:01:40.040
- [Walter] But as the movement took off,

00:01:40.040 --> 00:01:42.920
he needed to step back and
get out of the limelight

00:01:42.920 --> 00:01:45.660
for a while so that people
wouldn't be distracted

00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:48.120
by the fact that hew as gay.

00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:50.470
That was really the piece that kept him

00:01:50.470 --> 00:01:53.360
from rising in the movement
the way he could've

00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:54.413
had he not been.

00:01:58.620 --> 00:02:00.310
If you were to look at Bayard and me,

00:02:00.310 --> 00:02:02.640
on the surface, there
were great differences.

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:03.930
There was an age difference.

00:02:03.930 --> 00:02:05.610
There was a race difference.

00:02:05.610 --> 00:02:09.303
But we shared a deep bond and
a deep love for each other.

00:02:12.326 --> 00:02:13.593
In 1982, Bayard adopted
me so that we would have

00:02:16.100 --> 00:02:18.120
some legal protections under the law.

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:19.793
We were legal, father and son.

00:02:20.990 --> 00:02:24.310
This was a time when gay
couples had no protections,

00:02:24.310 --> 00:02:25.220
no rights.

00:02:25.220 --> 00:02:27.480
Other people were maybe a little shocked.

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:29.073
It didn't really matter to us.

00:02:32.456 --> 00:02:35.040
(serious music)

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:37.363
Bayard died in August of 1987.

00:02:38.290 --> 00:02:41.260
We decided to organize a
small, private foundation

00:02:41.260 --> 00:02:44.240
in his memory, the Bayard Rustin Fund.

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:47.340
It was really designed to get
Bayard into the history books.

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:49.850
He didn't have the
visibility of a Dr. King

00:02:49.850 --> 00:02:51.070
or of a Malcolm X.

00:02:51.070 --> 00:02:53.990
He would want to be
remembered for being a person

00:02:53.990 --> 00:02:56.930
that played a great role
in making social change

00:02:56.930 --> 00:02:57.833
in this country.

00:02:58.907 --> 00:03:01.574
(serious music)

